title NUL in an expect path

# NOT in the cross-parser diff yet, because the two parsers disagree
# and the C side is the one that is right.
#
# The reference decodes \000 in an expect path and ACCEPTS it, so the
# argument ends up holding a real NUL. The C side rejects it. Left
# alone, C would truncate the path at the NUL and compare a shorter
# string, silently -- and the leaf decoder already rejects NUL, so
# accepting it here is also inconsistent with the same parser's own
# treatment of the same byte.
#
# It is worth noting HOW this showed up. The reference's dump gains a
# raw NUL byte, so diff reports "binary files differ" rather than
# showing a line -- which is a loud way to be told that something is
# emitting a byte no text format should contain.
#
# Proposed message, matching the leaf one, which quotes nothing
# because a string holding a NUL cannot be quoted usefully:
#
#   expect path contains NUL, which no file name may hold
#
# Move this into devcheck/treecases/ once the reference rejects it.

tree base
  1-1: /
    2-100: plain

expect quarantined /a\000b
